Enzyme assays of extracellular proteins produced from pREV1 and pBluescript in E. coli revealed esterase activity towards r-nitrophenylacetate (Fig. 5
                        ). However, the enzymatic activity by the extracellular proteins from E. coli pREV1 (34.53 U mg−1±4.17) exhibited 1.5 times the esterase activity than those from E. colipBluescript (13.06 U mg−1±3.28). When the extracellular proteins from the twoisolates were subjected to a radial diffusion assay on an agarose plate containing polyurethane, onlythe protein from E. coli pREV1 displayed PUase as observed by a zone of clearing (Fig. 6
                        ). In addition, the esterase activity of E. coli pREV1 was inhibited 47% by the addition of1 mM phenylmethylsulfonylfluoride. No protease activity was observed against hide powder azurefrom either isolate.
